WEBVTT

NOTE Visual text from a silent video.

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:07.400
Training and inference require compute and physical capacity.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:14.100
When jobs arrive faster than they are served, the queue grows.

3
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:22.000
Adding chips is not enough when the power supply remains limited.

4
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:29.400
Power, water, land, permits, heat and latency constrain projects differently.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:36.100
A completed building cannot resolve a pending grid connection by itself.

6
00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:44.000
Check each condition; do not add resources with different units.

7
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:51.400
Launch cost depends on mass and price per kilogram.

8
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:58.100
Lower that price in the example: only part of the budget changes.

9
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:08.000
Panels, radiators, links and operations still have a cost.

10
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:15.400
The processor is only part of the mass sent into orbit.

11
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:22.100
Add power generation, thermal control, structure and communications.

12
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:30.000
Evaluate the mass of the whole system, not just the accelerator.

13
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:37.400
When data originates in orbit, processing there may reduce downlink traffic.

14
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:44.100
When it originates on Earth, moving the job adds an uplink and a downlink.

15
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:52.000
Compare both paths for the same user need.

16
00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:59.400
An economic forecast depends on launch, mass and lifetime assumptions.

17
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:06.100
Change one assumption and calculate the budget again.

18
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:14.000
Do not present the optimistic scenario as an achieved price.

